Harvest-aid application is an important operation in mechanized cotton production in Xinjiang. The progression of defoliation and boll opening directly affects harvest timing and picking quality, making timely assessment of defoliation rate and boll opening rate essential for determining when cotton is suitable for mechanical harvesting. Cotton production in Xinjiang covers large areas, with a limited harvest window and concentrated harvesting operations. Current practice still relies largely on manual sampling and experience-based judgment, which are labor-intensive and provide limited coverage of within-field variation in maturity. Drone-based visible-light remote sensing provides a means of rapidly acquiring canopy information over large cotton fields. Focusing on cotton at maturity, this dissertation examines the relationships between visible-image features and the two indicators and develops lightweight joint recognition and low-light enhancement methods to address canopy–background interference, differences in discriminative cues between the indicators, information degradation under low illumination, and limited computing resources on field devices. Predictions are linked to flight positions and acquisition times, and the methods are evaluated in production fields. The main research contributions and findings are as follows:

(1) A multi-temporal cotton image dataset with labels for both defoliation rate and boll opening rate was constructed, and the capacity of visible-image features to represent the two maturity indicators was examined. Drone imagery was linked to manual field surveys to assign grade labels for both indicators to each image sample. To address background interference and poor classification performance when using high-dimensional pixel inputs directly, structured features based on color and visible-light index statistics were constructed and compared with pixel-vector representations. The highest macro-F1 scores obtained with structured features were 0.867 for defoliation rate and 0.830 for boll opening rate, both exceeding the pixel-vector baselines. Feature analysis showed that changes in canopy greenness were closely associated with both maturity grades, while color statistics remained sensitive to illumination degradation.

(2) A low-light data synthesis and lightweight enhancement method was proposed for agricultural drone imagery. To address the difficulty of obtaining precisely paired low-light and normally exposed images and the shortage of agricultural training data, a controllable degradation model inspired by the imaging process was constructed. Its parameters were calibrated using real low-light images to generate paired training samples. A lightweight enhancement network, $L^3$-AgriUAVNet, was then designed to combine local detail extraction with global color and brightness adjustment, balancing canopy structure visibility and computational cost. With 0.139 M parameters, the model achieved a structural similarity index of 0.893 on the synthetic paired test set and improved the visibility of vegetation in dark regions and inter-row structures in real low-light images. In maize-seedling detection using real low-light images, enhancement increased the mAP\@0.5 of YOLOv8n by 14.6% relative to the original input, demonstrating a benefit for this lightweight detector; enhancement gains varied with detector configuration.

(3) A lightweight joint recognition network, RTCMNet, was designed to balance recognition performance and computation on edge devices. Defoliation and boll opening share canopy-level visual cues but differ in their local discriminative features. RTCMNet therefore uses a shared backbone and task-specific classification heads, together with multi-scale convolutional attention to capture information about residual leaves, exposed cotton lint, and canopy density at different spatial scales. In five-fold cross-validation within the target operating domain, mean accuracy and macro-F1 across the two tasks were both $0.93 plus.minus 0.02$, approaching the performance of larger comparison models. The network contains 0.37 M parameters and requires 32 ms per image for inference on a drone remote controller. The model produces both maturity indicators at a low computational cost, supporting rapid field recognition.

(4) A workflow for monitoring defoliation rate and boll opening rate in production cotton fields was established. Drone image predictions were linked to flight positions and acquisition times to generate maps of both indicators at different stages after harvest-aid application. Field results showed an overall increase in both indicators as maturation progressed, alongside local differences in the progression of defoliation and boll opening. Some areas of delayed maturity near field boundaries corresponded to field observations. Under low-light conditions at sunset, the enhancement and recognition models were cascaded to perform image processing, prediction of both indicators, and field mapping. In CPU tests conducted on the same computer, the cascaded models required an average of 167.6 ms to process a single $224 times 224$-pixel image patch, demonstrating the inference efficiency of the workflow under limited computing resources. These results provide a basis for locating and verifying within-field differences in maturity.

This dissertation develops methods for estimating defoliation and boll opening grades from drone-based visible-light imagery and mapping within-field differences in maturity to monitor cotton responses to harvest-aid application. Combining lightweight recognition with low-light processing provides a practical approach to analyzing cotton field imagery acquired at different times of day while reducing reliance on powerful computing hardware. The resulting methods can assist in planning field surveys and identifying areas of delayed maturity, providing a reference for evaluating harvest-aid effects and determining mechanical harvest timing.
